WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.184 --> 00:00:12.350
SARAH WAKEMAN: When I think about the opioid crisis,

00:00:12.350 --> 00:00:16.320
I think what we're really talking about are deaths due to opioid overdose.

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:20.000
And so an overdose can occur really anytime anyone uses an opioid.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:22.730
And when I say opioid, that's really an umbrella term

00:00:22.730 --> 00:00:27.570
that includes things like painkillers, prescription painkillers so Vicodin,

00:00:27.570 --> 00:00:31.160
Percocet, Oxycodone-- all the way to heroin

00:00:31.160 --> 00:00:34.940
and actually the opium poppy, which is where originally these substances were

00:00:34.940 --> 00:00:36.220
derived from.

00:00:36.220 --> 00:00:37.880
So that's the big term.

00:00:37.880 --> 00:00:40.610
And what we've seen in Massachusetts and across the country

00:00:40.610 --> 00:00:43.370
has been this dramatic rise in unintentional death

00:00:43.370 --> 00:00:46.312
from overdose on these drugs or medications.

00:00:46.312 --> 00:00:48.020
And while, again, this can happen anytime

00:00:48.020 --> 00:00:51.125
someone uses an opioid, most often when we see these deaths,

00:00:51.125 --> 00:00:53.950
it's due to untreated opioid addiction.

00:00:53.950 --> 00:00:58.440
So what we're really talking about right now is a crisis of opioid addiction.

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:00.710
And one of the reasons that's so devastating,

00:01:00.710 --> 00:01:02.570
and all of these deaths are so devastating,

00:01:02.570 --> 00:01:05.630
is that opioid addiction is a completely treatable illness.

00:01:05.630 --> 00:01:08.030
And so really, no one should be dying from an overdose.

00:01:08.030 --> 00:01:11.390
Because we have things we can do in the setting of an overdose

00:01:11.390 --> 00:01:15.170
to save someone's life and to keep people safe while they're actually

00:01:15.170 --> 00:01:16.610
in the throes of addiction.

00:01:16.610 --> 00:01:19.490
And we have treatment that we know reduces the death toll

00:01:19.490 --> 00:01:21.470
and helps people get into long-term remission

00:01:21.470 --> 00:01:23.900
to lead meaningful lives that really shouldn't

00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:25.970
be foreshortened by this disease.

00:01:25.970 --> 00:01:34.580
MARK ALBANESE: Every day, I encounter people in recovery who inspire me

00:01:34.580 --> 00:01:36.650
and who really give me hope around this.

00:01:36.650 --> 00:01:42.710
I think a lot of what we hear about in the media

00:01:42.710 --> 00:01:48.710
is people who aren't doing well, people who are tragically dying.

00:01:48.710 --> 00:01:52.430
Most of the people that I have the privilege to work with

00:01:52.430 --> 00:01:58.190
are really making great strides in their lives.

00:01:58.190 --> 00:02:01.010
It's an inspiring thing to watch.

00:02:01.010 --> 00:02:05.222
And I feel privileged to be a part of that.

00:02:05.222 --> 00:02:08.160
And it really gives me great hope for the future.

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:14.720
SHELLY GREENFIELD: The opioid crisis began about 15 years ago

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:19.550
when we saw an increase in the prescription

00:02:19.550 --> 00:02:23.570
of opioid analgesics or narcotic analgesics,

00:02:23.570 --> 00:02:26.750
or sometimes referred to as pain relievers.

00:02:26.750 --> 00:02:31.370
Because we saw such an explosion of prescriptions, what we found

00:02:31.370 --> 00:02:36.920
was that more people than ever before were exposed to opioids.

00:02:36.920 --> 00:02:40.490
In past generations, not very many people were exposed to opioids.

00:02:40.490 --> 00:02:46.850
But with this surge in prescribing, with a genuine intention of relieving pain,

00:02:46.850 --> 00:02:52.840
those who have a vulnerability to becoming dependent on opioids

00:02:52.840 --> 00:02:56.900
or developing an addiction problem on opioids were now exposed.

00:02:56.900 --> 00:02:59.730
MIKE DUGGAN: With the prescription opioid pain medication,

00:02:59.730 --> 00:03:02.420
it seemed like there was an abundance of these drugs everywhere.

00:03:02.420 --> 00:03:05.720
I had a lot of friends that also had injuries

00:03:05.720 --> 00:03:07.350
that were being prescribed medication.

00:03:07.350 --> 00:03:12.290
It just seemed like it was easier for my generation, as an underage person,

00:03:12.290 --> 00:03:15.990
to get access to a legal prescription narcotics on the streets

00:03:15.990 --> 00:03:18.590
than to get somebody to buy you alcohol because

00:03:18.590 --> 00:03:20.100
of the accessibility of these drugs.

00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:22.100
SCOTT WEINER: The problem that we have currently

00:03:22.100 --> 00:03:25.820
is that practitioners are literally all over the map.

00:03:25.820 --> 00:03:29.480
Some people will not prescribe anything for any condition,

00:03:29.480 --> 00:03:35.300
and some people will give 30, 60, 90 pills of oxycodone or hydrocodone.

00:03:35.300 --> 00:03:37.370
And so I think what's happening is that we're

00:03:37.370 --> 00:03:40.400
creating this severe problem of expectations

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:43.040
for patients, because they don't know what

00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:45.410
they're going to get from which doctor.

00:03:45.410 --> 00:03:47.120
We need to do a better job with that.

00:03:47.120 --> 00:03:49.370
EFRAIN LOZADA: I pretty much started using drugs--

00:03:49.370 --> 00:03:56.880
actually, I started drinking alcohol before the age of 14.

00:03:56.880 --> 00:04:01.550
And then eventually, from there, I progressed into snorting cocaine.

00:04:01.550 --> 00:04:06.140
And it was probably around the age of maybe 20

00:04:06.140 --> 00:04:07.821
that I started snorting heroin.

00:04:07.821 --> 00:04:10.070
PATRICK GLYNN: The opioid crisis is twofold, actually.

00:04:10.070 --> 00:04:13.460
It's prescription medication and also powder heroin and now

00:04:13.460 --> 00:04:16.550
the introduction of fentanyl and various forms of that.

00:04:16.550 --> 00:04:20.500
So it's taken on two folds where we have to attack it from both ends.

00:04:20.500 --> 00:04:22.880
MIKE DUGGAN: The opioid crisis is affecting everybody.

00:04:22.880 --> 00:04:24.090
It's in every community.

00:04:24.090 --> 00:04:26.410
It doesn't discriminate against anybody.

00:04:26.410 --> 00:04:27.920
It doesn't matter poverty.

00:04:27.920 --> 00:04:30.600
Doesn't matter race.

00:04:30.600 --> 00:04:32.790
It doesn't matter where you come from.

00:04:32.790 --> 00:04:36.230
Every family is affected by it.

